## Signal Detection Theory for recognition memory {.section-divider}

## Two distributions, one decision


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![](01_Intro_bmm_SDT_files/figure-revealjs/unnamed-chunk-8-1.png){fig-alt='Two overlapping evidence distributions for new and old items with a criterion line; the areas right of the criterion mark false alarms and hits, and the distance between the distribution means is labeled d-prime.' width=960}
:::
:::


::: {.small}
$d'$ — how far the distributions sit apart (memory) · $c$ — how much evidence
it takes to say "old" (bias)
:::

::: {.notes}
Every response the task records falls out of this picture: old item right of
the criterion is a hit, new item right of it is a false alarm. Two numbers
describe the whole observer — separation and threshold. Next slide moves the
threshold.
:::

## Move the criterion — accuracy moves, memory doesn't {.smaller}

Same d' (memory), two criteria. Watch what moves — and what doesn't:


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![](01_Intro_bmm_SDT_files/figure-revealjs/unnamed-chunk-9-1.png){fig-alt='Two panels with identical new-item and old-item evidence distributions but different criterion placements; hit rate, false-alarm rate, and accuracy differ between panels although d-prime is identical.' width=960}
:::
:::


::: {.notes}
d' is fixed at 1.3 — memory never changes on this slide. Left panel: neutral
criterion. Right panel: liberal criterion — hits AND false alarms rise
together, accuracy shifts. Everything an accuracy analysis sees just moved
without any change in memory. Hold that thought for the real data.
:::

## The data: recognition memory {.smaller}

Koen, Aly, Wang, & Yonelinas (2013) — shipped with the MPTinR package:

- Study word lists → old/new recognition test with **6-point confidence
  ratings** (1 = *sure new* … 6 = *sure old*)
- Two **between-subjects** test conditions, 48 participants each:
  - `full_attention` — test under full attention (Exp. 2)
  - `immediate` — test immediately after study (Exp. 4)
- 23,902 trials total; codebook in `data/README.md`


